H^i_{dR}(V) := \mathbb{H}^i(V, \Omega^{\bullet}_{V/k}),  F^k H^i_{dR}(V) = \mathbb{H}^i(V, \Omega^{\geq k}_{V/k})

I_{\sigma}: H^i_{dR}(V) \otimes_{k,\sigma} \mathbb{C} \xrightarrow{\sim} H^i_B(V_{\sigma}, \mathbb{Q}) \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{C}

such that F^k H^i_{dR}(V) \otimes_{k,\sigma} \mathbb{C} on the de Rham side corresponds to \bigoplus_{p \geq k} H^{p,q} on the Betti side. If \sigma: k \to \mathbb{C} is a real embedding, then the conjugation 1 \otimes c on the de Rham side corresponds to \iota_{\sigma} \otimes c on the Betti side so that we have an isomorphism H^i_{dR}(V) \otimes_{k,\sigma} \mathbb{R} \cong (H^i_B(V_{\sigma}, \mathbb{Q}) \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{C})^{\iota_{\sigma} \otimes c} (see [2, Proposition 1.4]). ... let us introduce an involution c_{dR} on H^i_{dR}(V) \otimes_{k,\sigma} \mathbb{C} corresponding to 1 \otimes c under I_{\sigma}.

Theorem 2.1. [4] There is an isomorphism
\phi_C : \frac{A_{c_X} \otimes_{k,\sigma} \mathbb{C}}{Jac(S) \cap (A_{c_X} \otimes_{k,\sigma} \mathbb{C})} \stackrel{\sim}{\longrightarrow} H_0 = \bigoplus_{p+q=n-1} H_0^{p,q}
which sends the wt r homogeneous part A_{c_X,(r)} \otimes_{k,\sigma} \mathbb{C} (for r \leq n-1) to H_0^{n-1-r, r}.  Footnote: If r \geq n, then the wt r homogeneous part belongs to the Jacobian ideal.

\mathcal{R}([y^i f(x)], [y^j g(x)]) = \begin{cases} b_{i,j} (2\pi\sqrt{-1})^{n-1} e(e-1)^n r_{f(x)g(x)} & \text{if } i+j = n-1 \\ 0 & \text{if } i+j \ne n-1 \end{cases}
(2.11) b_{i,j} = (-1)^{\frac{i(i+1)}{2} + \frac{j(j+1)}{2} + j^2}
Note that the same formula would realize the "complex conjugation" of y^{n-1-i} \tilde{f}_{j_i} to be y^i f_{j_i} as it should be; the asymmetry in b_{i,j} cancels out the skew-symmetricity of Q.
